WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.670
"How to Get a Good Night's Sleep
Without Sleeping Pills"

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.410
In the Harvard Nurses' Health Study,
women who got 5 or fewer hours

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.110
of sleep a night gained
about 6 pounds more

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:21.400
over the subsequent 16 years
than those getting 7 a night.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.300
Even if that was all due
to the difference in sleep,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.090
that's still only 6 pounds
for 10,000+ more hours of sleep.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.559
If even a tiny fraction of that time
was spent on diet and exercise,

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:34.980
you know, biking
to the nearest farm stand,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.010
more weight could have been lost
in 16 weeks than in those 16 years.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.239
Every little bit helps, though,

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:44.520
and getting at least seven hours
is healthier for you anyway.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.300
This biggest reason to lose
sleep over losing sleep

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.140
is motor vehicle accident risk.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:56.129
Driving drowsy increases your risk
of killing yourself and others.

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:58.039
People might think twice
about getting behind the wheel

00:00:58.039 --> 00:01:00.239
after staying awake
for 48 hours straight,

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.989
but even just two weeks
of sleeping 6 hours a night

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:04.589
impairs your cognitive performance

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:07.979
as much as pulling two
all-nighters in a row.

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:11.619
So, what's the best way
to sleep better?

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:13.870
Sleeping pills are a nonstarter.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.880
Those prescribed even
less than 18 pills a year

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:17.880
of so-called hypnotics,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.860
the class of sleeping pills
that includes Ambien,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.070
appear to have triple
the risk of dying prematurely.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:26.590
Since up to 10%
of the adult population

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:27.740
is prescribed these drugs,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.869
if the drugs really are killing people,

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:33.200
that could mean a six-figure
death toll every year.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.259
Ambien's manufacturer
questioned the study,

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:38.470
but that's just one of dozens
of studies that found

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.750
a significant association

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.729
between sleeping pills
and premature death.

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:45.290
When the principal investigator
at the Scripps Clinic Sleep Center

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:49.100
was criticized for reporting
alarmingly high death risks

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.180
from these commonly
used medications,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.490
he replied: "We can't hide risks,
even if they might frighten patients

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.240
out of taking hypnotics.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.070
Patients have a right to know."

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.950
What's more, non-pharmacological
methods have been found

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.140
to work as good or
better than the drugs.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.759
The recommended first-line
treatment for insomnia

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:10.890
is what's called
"cognitive behavioral therapy, "

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.870
which combines
conditioning techniques

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.010
to re-associate
the bed with sleep

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.210
and education surrounding
optimal sleep hygiene.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.930
Here are the four rules
of sleep conditioning.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.540
(1) Go to bed only
when you're sleepy.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.530
(2) Only use the bed
for sleep (and sex).

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.370
No reading, eating,
or screen time.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.900
(3) If you can't fall asleep
within about 15-20 minutes,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.050
get up, leave the bedroom,
and don't come back

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.540
until you're sleepy again.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.340
And repeat as necessary.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.130
And finally (4), get up at
the same time every morning,

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.349
no matter how little sleep you get.

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:47.140
And although avoiding
napping is often added,

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.520
contrary to expectations,
the majority of research

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.050
does not show that daytime naps
interfere with nighttime sleep.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.360
If there were four rules
of sleep hygiene I'd pick out,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.060
it would be (1):
exercise regularly.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.190
The best time to exercise
to improve sleep

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.870
appears to be 4 to 8 hours
before bedtime,

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.990
though it appears to be a myth
that exercising right before bed

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.300
is somehow disruptive to sleep.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.470
(2) Avoid caffeine, nicotine,
and alcohol before bedtime.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.560
We used to think as long as
you don't drink caffeine

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.120
in the evening,
it wouldn't affect sleep,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.390
but four cups of coffee worth
even six hours before bedtime

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.480
can reduce total sleep time
by more than an hour.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.540
Even just two cups in the morning
at 7 a.m. can change

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.860
what your brainwaves
look like on EEG that night,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.620
though it's not clear if this
has any clinical relevance

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.430
beyond delaying the onset of sleep
by an average of 10 minutes.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:44.120
Late afternoon alcohol consumption
(6 hours before bedtime)

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.060
may also impair sleep, even after
it's cleared out of your system.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.250
And nicotine, whether from gum,
pill, patch, or cigarette, can also

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.620
have negative sleep effects,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.890
though so too can
active nicotine withdrawal.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.700
Food-wise, low fiber intakes and high
saturated fat and sugar intake

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.900
is associated with lighter,
less restorative sleep.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.920
Meat intake is associated
with napping,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.140
suggested as a proxy
for sleepiness.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.590
This may be one of the reasons
insomnia has been reported

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.530
as a side-effect of low carb,
ketogenic diets.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:17.530
Supplement-wise,

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.780
a single megadose of vitamin D
was found to improve

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.040
sleep duration and quality
in men and women

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.200
ages 20 through 50
with sleeping disorders.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.660
And I've already talked
about melatonin-rich foods

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.509
and supplements
in previous videos.

00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:36.810
(3) Make the bedroom quiet,
dark, cool, and comfortable.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.280
Nocturnal noise can
adversely impact sleep

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.900
even if we're not
consciously aware of it.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.280
Within a few days, you can
become habituated to noises

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.070
such that they don't wake you up,
but EEG studies and subjective

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:52.930
sleep surveys show the quality
of your sleep can still be affected.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:57.259
Thankfully, ear plugs and sound
masking, like white noise machines,

00:04:57.259 --> 00:04:59.160
have been shown to help.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:00.760
And finally (4),

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.590
establish a relaxing
bedtime routine.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:07.460
Relaxation techniques such as
massage, mindfulness meditation,

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.970
and soothing music may also help.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.180
So may taking a relaxing
hot bath or shower.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.790
One of the reasons late-night
eating can delay sleep

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:17.400
is that it may interfere
with the drop

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.150
in core body temperature that
normally occurs about bedtime.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.500
That's thought to be one
of the cues that it's time for bed.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.580
So, should you not take
a hot bath before bed?

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.470
No, because as soon as you
step out of the bath or shower,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:34.220
the rapid decline in skin
temperature can accentuate

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.699
the natural nighttime
drop and improve sleep.

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:38.930
Even just a warm foot bath

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:43.303
may help you fall asleep
about 15 minutes faster.

